window.__registerChapter({"num": 57, "slug": "057_Hereditary_Autoinflammatory_Disorders", "title": "Hereditary Autoinflammatory Disorders", "authors": "Yagmur Bayindir, Seza Ozen", "part_num": "X", "part_title": "Immune/Inflammatory Disorders", "word_count": 13126, "pearls": ["Systemic autoinflammatory diseases are rare genetic disorders characterized by recurrent episodes or continuous inflammation without evidence of autoimmunity.", "Autoinflammation is characterized by spontaneous activation of the innate immune system and activation of inflammatory pathways typically independent of an antigenic trigger.", "Autoimmune diseases, however, are characterized by self antigen activation of the adaptive immune system (i.e., T and B lymphocytes) due to a breakdown in self-tolerance.", "Autoinflammatory disorders were originally called periodic or recurrent fever syndromes, but the term autoinflammation was coined to include the expanding list of genetic disorders with spontaneous inflammation, some of which are not characterized by fevers.", "Familial mediterranean fever (FMF) is characterized by episodes of fever lasting 1 to 3 days, accompanied by sterile peritonitis and/or pleural inflammation, arthritis, and rarely an erysipelas-like rash.", "During episodes, young children may primarily experience fever, which is the most common symptom, whereas abdominal pain becomes more prominent as they grow older.", "A number of diagnostic criteria have been proposed for FMF; in the Eurofever/PRINTO (Pediatric Rheumatology INternational Trials Organization) endorsed criteria for a patient with a pathogenic variant for FMF (Table 57.1).", "Colchicine intolerance is common, often characterized by mild gastrointestinal symptoms (diarrhea and nausea)."], "genes": ["ACP5", "ADA2", "ADAR1", "ARPC1B", "CDC42", "CECR1", "CIAS1", "DNASE1", "FAM105B", "HA20", "HOIL1", "IFIH1", "IL10RA", "IL10RB", "IL1RN", "IL36RN", "ISG15", "LACC1"], "omims": [], "subtopics": ["Hereditary Autoinflammatory Disorders YAGMUR BAYINDIR, SEZA OZEN", "Autoinflammatory Diseases", "The Monogenic Autoinflammatory Diseases—cont'd", "IL-1 Related Autoinflammatory Diseases and Inflammasomopathies", "Familial Mediterranean Fever", "Pyrin Associated Autoinflammation With Neutrophilic Dermatosis (PAAND)", "Pyogenic Arthritis With Pyoderma Gangrenosum and Acne (PAPA) Syndrome", "Cryopyrin-Associated Periodic Syndromes (CAPS)", "NLRP12-Associated Autoinflammatory Disease/Guadeloupe Variant Periodic Fever Syndrome (FCAS2)", "Deficiency of Interleukin-1 Receptor Antagonist (DIRA)", "Majeed Syndrome", "IL-18 Related Autoinflammatory Diseases", "Autoinflammation With Infantile Enterocolitis (AIFEC)/NLRC4-AID", "Mevalonate Kinase Deficiency/ Hyperimmunoglobulinemia D and Periodic Fever Syndrome"], "figs": [{"img": "fig_p1280_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 57.1", "caption": "Autoinflammatory diseases mediated by cell death pathways and inflammasomes. (A) The TNF-mediated cell death pathway is activated by stimulation of TNFR1 by TNF, followed by formation of complex I, consisting of trimerized TNFR1, TRADD, RIPK1, TRAF2, and TRAF5. This complex is regulated by K63 or linear ubiquitination, which is controlled by a set of E3 ligases and deubiquitinases, including cIAP1/cIAP2, LUBAC, A20, and OTULIN. NEMO and TBK1 are recruited to the linear ubiquitin chains on RIPK1 to take part in the conduction of NF-κB signaling. In the setting of NF-κB inhibition, RIPK1 is activated and binds with FADD and caspase-8, leading to complex IIa and complex IIb formation, where RIPK1 is cleaved by caspase-8 to execute apoptosis. If the cleavage of RIPK1 by caspase-8 is impaired, RIPK1 undergoes dimerization via the C-terminal death domain, leading to its activation and subsequent interaction with RIPK3 and MLKL to form the complex IIb (necrosome), which promotes necroptosis. (B) NLRP1, NLRP3, pyrin, and NLRC4 inflammasomes are regulated by various proteins, including DPP9, WDR1, PSTPIP1, MVK, and CDC42. After activation of inflammasomes, procaspase-1 is self-activated and cleaves several substrates, including the proinflammatory cytokines IL-1β and IL-18. GSDMD is another substrate of caspase-1, whose cleavage leads to the liberation of its N-terminal domain to form pores in plasma membranes and causes a type of inflammatory cell death termed pyropt"}, {"img": "fig_p1282_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 57.2", "caption": "Cutaneous manifestations of inherited autoinflammatory disorders. (A) Annular erythematous plaques studded with pustules in an infant with deficiency of the interleukin-1 receptor antagonist (DIRA); note the similarity to pustular psoriasis. (B) Child with deficiency of interleukin-36 receptor antagonist (DITRA)—widespread psoriasiform plaques whose edges contain pustules. (C) Blau syndrome with numerous, small, pink-tan, flat papules in a generalized distribution in a 1-year-old boy; histologically, a granulomatous infiltrate is seen. (D) STING (STimulator of INterferon Genes)-associated vasculopathy with onset in infancy (SAVI) is characterized by acral violaceous plaques and resorption of digits and loss of nails; patients may be diagnosed as having Aicardi–Goutières syndrome, antiphospholipid antibody syndrome, or vasculitis. (E) Aicardi–Goutières syndrome with pernio-like lesions. (F) Chronic atypical neutrophilic dermatosis with lipodystrophy and elevated temperature (CANDLE) syndrome with deforming arthritis (e.g., swan neck deformity) and thin pink plaques overlying the metacarpophalangeal joints that resemble dermatomyositis. (G) Livedo racemosa in a patient with adenosine deaminase 2 (ADA2) deficiency. (H) Cystic acne and extensive scarring in a pediatric patient with pyogenic arthritis, pyoderma gangrenosum, and acne (PAPA) syndrome. (A, Courtesy Gillian Clarke, MD. B, Courtesy Antonio Torrelo, MD. C,E, Courtesy Julie V. Schaffer, MD. D,F–H, Courte"}, {"img": "fig_p1286_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 57.3", "caption": "Mechanism of the NLRP3 inflammasome pathway. NLRP3 inflammasomes are formed by priming and activation signaling. In the priming signaling, several pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs) or danger-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs) bind to the pattern-recognition receptors (PRRs) such as Toll-like receptors and activate the NF-κB signaling pathway, which leads to the upregulation of NLRP3, pro-interleukin (IL)-1β, and pro-IL-18. In the activation signaling, various PAMPs, DAMPs, or intracellular changes induce the formation of the NLRP3 inflammasome composed of NLRP3 as a PRR, pro-caspase-1, and adapter proteins such as the apoptosis-associated speck-like protein containing a caspase recruitment domain that connects NLRP3 and pro-caspase-1. Then, the inflammasome complex activates pro-caspase-1. The activated caspase-1 cleaves the pro-IL-1β and pro-IL-18 into IL-1β and IL-18, which are biologically active forms. ASC, Apoptosis-associated speck-like protein containing a caspase recruitment domain; DAMP, danger-associated molecular pattern; IL, interleukin; NF- κB, nuclear factor kappa B; NLRP3, NOD-like receptor family pyrin domain containing 3; TLR, Toll-like receptor."}, {"img": "fig_p1294_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 57.4", "caption": "Overview of ubiquitination/deubiquitination within NF-κB pathways. Signaling through NF-κB pathways is regulated by ubiquitination/deubiquitination. Addition of polyubiquitin chains to various pathway components can serve as a mediator for activation while marking others for degradation. LUBAC is an activator of the canonical pathway. It is a complex made up of 3 components: HOIL-1, HOIP, and SHANK-associated RH domain interactor (SHARPIN). On receptor ligation, LUBAC adds polyubiquitin chains to targets, such as NEMO and RIPK1, leading to IKK activation through TAK1. Deubiquitination by enzymes, such as OTULIN and A20, plays a vital role in extinguishing signaling through the NF-κB pathway, maintaining cellular homeostasis. linear-Ub , Met1-linked ubiquitin; K63-Ub , Lys63-linked ubiquitin."}, {"img": "vfig_p1293_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 57.4", "caption": "Overview of ubiquitination/deubiquitination within NF-κB pathways. Signaling through NF-κB pathways is regulated by ubiquitination/deubiquitination. Addition of polyubiquitin chains to various pathway components can serve as a mediator for activation while marking others for degradation. LUBAC is an activator of the canonical pathway. It is a complex made up of 3 components: HOIL-1, HOIP, and SHANK-associated RH domain interactor (SHARPIN). On receptor ligation, LUBAC adds polyubiquitin chains to targets, such as NEMO and RIPK1, leading to IKK activation through TAK1. Deubiquitination by enzymes, such as OTULIN and A20, plays a vital role in extinguishing signaling through the NF-κB pathway, maintaining cellular homeostasis. linear-Ub , Met1-linked ubiquitin; K63-Ub , Lys63-linked ubiquitin."}], "tables_meta": [{"label": "Table 57.1", "title": "CAPS FMF TRAPS MKD"}, {"label": "Table 57.2", "title": "PFAPA CAPS FMF TRAPS MKD"}, {"label": "TABLE 57.3", "title": "The Monogenic Al utoinflammat lory Diseases"}, {"label": "TABLE 57.3", "title": "The Monogenic l Autoinflamma ltory Diseases—c l ont'd"}, {"label": "TABLE 57.5", "title": "#### The Monogenic Autoinflammatory Diseases—cont'd"}, {"label": "TABLE 57.4", "title": "Variants to Be Te for FMF sted in theMEFV Gene"}], "body_md": "\n<!-- p.1278 -->\n\n# 57 Hereditary Autoinflammatory Disorders **YAGMUR BAYINDIR, SEZA OZEN** \n\n## **Autoinflammatory Diseases** \n\nSystemic autoinflammatory diseases are rare genetic disorders characterized by recurrent episodes or continuous inflammation without evidence of autoimmunity. _Autoinflammation_ is characterized by spontaneous activation of the innate immune system and activation of inflammatory pathways typically independent of an antigenic trigger. _Autoimmune_ diseases, however, are characterized by self antigen activation of the adaptive immune system (i.e., T and B lymphocytes) due to a breakdown in self-tolerance. Autoinflammatory disorders were originally called periodic or recurrent fever syndromes, but the term autoinflammation was coined to include the expanding list of genetic disorders with spontaneous inflammation, some of which are not characterized by fevers. The distinction between autoinflammation and autoimmunity is sometimes not very clear, and some of these disorders have both autoinflammatory and autoimmune features. In general autoinflammatory disorders do not exhibit increased risk of infections, but there are several disorders with autoinflammatory characteristics as well as an immunodeficiency phenotype, which further complicates the definition and classification of autoinflammatory diseases. \n\nAutoinflammatory disorders can be caused by genetic defects resulting in either gain-of-function of a proinflammatory protein or loss-of-function of an inhibitory protein (Fig. 57.1). Regardless of the specific mechanism, the outcome is the continuous activation of the innate immune system and release of inflammatory cytokines. This persistent immune activation leads to common clinical symptoms, including fever, skin rash, arthritis, gastroint estinal dysfunction, and other end-organ damage. \n\nThe first autoinflammatory disorders where pathophysiological mechanisms were elucidated were linked to the production of biologically active interleukin-1β (IL-1). However the spectrum of autoinflammatory disorders has expanded and their underlying mechanisms are better understood which has shown that these disorders can result from the activation of various inflammatory pathways. \n\nAutoinflammatory disorders can thus be classified according to pathogenic mechanisms such as disorders driven by IL-1 production including FMF (familial Mediterranean fever), CAPS (cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes) and HIDS (hyperimmunoglobulinemia D syndrome); diseases linked to NF-κB activation as in Blau syndrome and haploinsufficiency of A20 (HA20); those due to interferon excess as in SAVI (STING-associated vasculopathy with onset in infancy) and CANDLE (chronic atypical neutrophilic dermatosis with lipodystrophy and elevated temperature); and those with unclear pathophysiological mechanisms such as TRAPS (tumor necrosis factor receptor-associated periodic syndrome) and deficiency of adenosine deaminase-2 (DADA2). \n\nSystemic autoinflammatory diseases often present with recurring episodes of fever, serositis, arthritis, and skin inflammation that occur without an external trigger. Inflammation in muscles, vasculitis affecting small, medium and large vessels, and systemic amyloidosis may also occur in some patients. The idea that the inflammatory symptoms in autoinflammatory diseases are entirely unprovoked is a matter of context, as we are aware that several factors can trigger episodes such as psychological stress, trauma, vaccinations, and exposure to cold. Because of their genetic basis, the majority of these disorders typically manifest early in life, with symptoms emerging anywhere from the first hours after birth to the first decade of life. Only a small subset of patients experience the onset of symptoms during adulthood. \n\nSystemic autoinflammatory diseases should be considered as a potential diagnosis when a patient displays unexplained inflammatory symptoms impacting the mucocutaneous, gastrointestinal, and/or musculoskeletal systems along with laboratory evidence of systemic inflammation (e.g., elevated C-reactive protein (CRP) levels and/or neutrophilic leukocytosis). It is critical to exclude infections, neoplasms, and classic autoimmune diseases in these patients. The diagnosis of autoinflammatory disorders primarily relies on comprehensive history taking, including a detailed family history, and clinical judgement (Tables 57.1 and 57.2). The recurring presence of consistent clinical\n\n<!-- p.1279 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p1280_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 57.1** Autoinflammatory diseases mediated by cell death pathways and inflammasomes. (A) The TNF-mediated cell death pathway is activated by stimulation of TNFR1 by TNF, followed by formation of complex I, consisting of trimerized TNFR1, TRADD, RIPK1, TRAF2, and TRAF5. This complex is regulated by K63 or linear ubiquitination, which is controlled by a set of E3 ligases and deubiquitinases, including cIAP1/cIAP2, LUBAC, A20, and OTULIN. NEMO and TBK1 are recruited to the linear ubiquitin chains on RIPK1 to take part in the conduction of NF-κB signaling. In the setting of NF-κB inhibition, RIPK1 is activated and binds with FADD and caspase-8, leading to complex IIa and complex IIb formation, where RIPK1 is cleaved by caspase-8 to execute apoptosis. If the cleavage of RIPK1 by caspase-8 is impaired, RIPK1 undergoes dimerization via the C-terminal death domain, leading to its activation and subsequent interaction with RIPK3 and MLKL to form the complex IIb (necrosome), which promotes necroptosis. (B) NLRP1, NLRP3, pyrin, and NLRC4 inflammasomes are regulated by various proteins, including DPP9, WDR1, PSTPIP1, MVK, and CDC42. After activation of inflammasomes, procaspase-1 is self-activated and cleaves several substrates, including the proinflammatory cytokines IL-1β and IL-18. GSDMD is another substrate of caspase-1, whose cleavage leads to the liberation of its N-terminal domain to form pores in plasma membranes and causes a type of inflammatory cell death termed pyroptosis. _A20,_ TNF alpha–induced protein 3; _AIFEC,_ autoinflammation with infantile enterocolitis; _ASC,_ apoptosis-associated speck-like protein containing a caspase recruitment domain; _CAPS,_ cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes; _CDC42,_ cell division cycle 42; _cIAP,_ cellular inhibitor of apoptosis protein; _CRIA,_ cleavage-resistant RIPK1–induced autoinflammatory; _DAMP,_ damage-associated molecular pattern; _DPP9,_ dipeptidyl peptidase 9; _FADD,_ Fas-associated via death domain; _FCAS,_ familial cold autoinflammatory syndrome; _FKLC,_ familial keratosis lichenoides chronica; _FMF,_ familial Mediterranean fever; _GSDMD,_ gasdermin-D; _HA20,_ haploinsufficiency of A20; _HIDS,_ hyperimmunoglobulin D syndrome; _HOIL1,_ heme-oxidized IRP2 ubiquitin ligase-1; _HOIP,_ HOIL1-interacting protein; _IL,_ interleukin; _LUBAC,_ linear ubiquitin chain assembly complex; _MLKL,_ mixed-lineage kinase domain-like pseudokinase; _MSPC,_ multiple self-healing palmoplantar carcinoma; _MVK,_ mevalonate kinase; _NAIAD,_ NLRP1-associated autoinflammation with arthritis and dyskeratosis; _NDAS,_ NEMO deleted exon 5 autoinflammatory syndrome; _NEMO,_ NF-κB essential modulator; _NF-kB_ , nuclear factor kappa B; _NLRC4,_ NLR family CARD domaincontaining 4; _NLRP3,_ NLR family pyrin domain-containing 3; _NOCARH,_ neonatal onset of cytopenia, autoinflammation, rash, and hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis; _OTULIN,_ OTU deubiquitinase with linear linkage specificity; _PAAND,_ pyrin-associated autoinflammation with neutrophilic dermatosis; _PAID,_ PSTPIP1-associated inflammatory disease; _PAMP,_ pathogen-associated molecular pattern; _PFITS,_ periodic fever, immunodeficiency, and thrombocytopenia syndrome; _PSTPIP1,_ proline-serine-threonine phosphatase–interacting protein 1; _RIPK1,_ receptor-interacting serine/ threonine kinase 1; _RIPK3,_ receptor-interacting serine/threonine kinase 3; _SHARPIN,_ SHANK-associated RH domain–interacting protein; _TBK1,_ TANK-binding kinase 1; _TNF,_ tumor necrosis factor; _TNFR1,_ tumor necrosis factor receptor 1; _TRADD,_ TNFRSF1Aassociated via death domain; _TRAF,_ tumor necrosis factor receptor-associated factor; _TRAPS,_ tumor necrosis factor receptor-associated periodic syndrome; _Ub,_ ubiquitination; _WDR1,_ WD repeat domain 1; _ZBP1,_ Z-DNA-binding protein 1.  (From Zhang J, Lee PY, Aksentijevich I, Ahou Q. How to build a fire: the genetics of autoinflammatory diseases. _Ann Rev Genet_ . 2023;57:245-274 [Fig. 2, P.248].)\n\n<!-- p.1280 -->\n\n#### **TABLE Eurofever/PRINTO Classification Criteria for Hereditary Recurrent Fevers and Their Performance in the 57.1 281 Patients With Consensus** \n\n|CAPS|FMF|TRAPS|MKD|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Presence of a_confirmatory_<br>_NLRP3 genotype_*and<br>_at least one_among the<br>following:<br>• Urticarial rash<br>• Red eye (conjunctivitis,<br>episcleritis, uveitis)<br>• Neurosensorial hearing loss|Presence of_confirmatory MEFV_<br>_genotype_*and_at least one_<br>among the following:<br>• Duration of episodes 1–3 days<br>• Arthritis<br>• Chest pain<br>• Abdominal pain|Presence of confirmatory<br>_TNFRSF1A_genotype*and<br>at least one among the<br>following:<br>• Duration of episodes≥7 days<br>• Myalgia<br>• Migratory rash<br>• Periorbital oedema<br>• Relatives affected|Presence of a_confirmatory_<br>_MVK genotype_*and<br>_at least one_among the<br>following<br>• Gastrointestinal<br>symptoms<br>• Cervical lymphadenitis<br>• Aphthous stomatitis|\n|OR|OR|OR||\n|Presence of_not confirmatory_<br>_NLRP3 genotype_<sup>†</sup>and<br>_at least two_among the<br>following:<br>• Urticarial rash<br>• Red eye (conjunctivitis,<br>episcleritis, uveitis)<br>• Neurosensorial hearing loss|Presence of not_confirmatory_<br>_MEFV_genotype<sup>‡</sup>and at least<br>two among the following:<br>• Duration of episodes 1–3 days<br>• Arthritis<br>• Chest pain<br>• Abdominal pain|Presence of a not confirmatory<br>_TNFRSF1A_genotype<sup>†</sup>and<br>_at least two_among the<br>following:<br>• Duration of episodes≥7 days<br>• Myalgia<br>• Migratory rash<br>• Periorbital oedema<br>• Relatives affected||\n|Sensitivity: 1|Sensitivity: 0.94|Sensitivity: 0.95|Sensitivity: 0.98|\n|Specificity: 1|Specificity: 0.95|Specificity: 0.99|Specificity: 1|\n|Accuracy: 1|Accuracy: 0.98|Accuracy: 0.99|Accuracy: 1|\n\n\nA patient with (1) evidence of elevation of acute phase reactants (ESR or CRP or SAA) in correspondence to the clinical flares and (2) careful consideration of possible confounding diseases (neoplasms, infections, autoimmune conditions, other inborn errors of immunity) and a reasonable period of recurrent disease activity (at least 6 months) is classified as having hereditary recurrent fever if the criteria are met. \n\n*Pathogenic or likely pathogenic variants (heterozygous in AD diseases, homozygous or in trans (or biallelic) compound heterozygous in AR diseases). †Variant of uncertain significance (VUS). Benign and likely benign variants should be excluded. \n\n> ‡In trans compound heterozygous for one pathogenic _MEFV_ variants and one VUS, or biallelic VUS, or heterozygous for one pathogenic _MEFV_ variant. _AD,_ Autosomal dominant, _AR,_ autosomal recessive; _CAPS,_ cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes; _CRP,_ C-reactive protein, _ESR,_ erythrocyte sedimentation rate; _FMF,_ familial Mediterranean fever, _MKD,_ mevalonate kinase deficiency; _MVK,_ mevalonate kinase; _PRINTO,_ pediatric rheumatology international trial organization; _SAA,_ serum amyloid A; _TRAPS,_ tumor necrosis factor receptor-associated periodic fever syndrome. From Gattorno M, Hofer M, Federici S, et al. Classification criteria for autoinflammatory recurrent fevers. _Ann Rheum Dis_ . 2019;78:1025-1032 (Table 2, p. 1028). \n\n#### **TABLE Eurofever/PRINTO Clinical Classification Criteria for PFAPA and Hereditary Recurrent Fevers and 57.2 Their Performance in the 281 for Whom Consensus Was Achieved** \n\n|PFAPA|CAPS|FMF|TRAPS|MKD|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|At least seven out of<br>eight:|Presence of at least two<br>of five*:|At least six out of nine:<br>Presence|Score≥5 points<br>Presence|Presence of_at least_<br>_three of six_.|\n|Presence|• Urticarial rash|• Eastern Mediterranean|• Fever≥7 days (2 points)|• Age at onset|\n|• Pharyngotonsillitis|• Cold/stress-triggered|ethnicity|• Fever 5–6 days (1 point)|<1 years|\n|• Duration of<br>episodes, 3–6 days|episodes<br>• Sensorineural|• Duration of episodes,<br>1–3 days|• Migratory rash (1 point)<br>• Periorbital oedema (1 point)|• Gastrointestinal<br>symptoms|\n|• Cervical|hearing loss|• Chest pain|• Myalgia (1 point)|• Painful lymph|\n|lymphadenitis|• Chronic aseptic|• Abdominal pain|• Positive family history|nodes|\n|• Periodicity|meningitis|• Arthritis|(1 point)|• Aphthous|\n|Absence|• Skeletal abnormalities|Absence|Absence|stomatitis|\n|• Diarrhea|(epiphysial|• Aphthous stomatitis|• Aphthous stomatitis|• Triggers|\n|• Chest pain|overgrowth/frontal|• Urticarial rash|(1 point)|• Maculopapular|\n|• Skin rash<br>• Arthritis|bossing)|• Maculopapular rash<br>• Painful lymph nodes|• Pharyngotonsillitis<br>(1 point)|rash|\n|Sensitivity: 0.97|Sensitivity: 0.80|Sensitivity: 0.91|Sensitivity: 0.87|Sensitivity: 0.91|\n|Specificity: 0.93|Specificity: 0.91|Specificity: 0.92|Specificity: 0.92|Specificity: 0.82|\n|Accuracy: 0.99|Accuracy: 0.85|Accuracy: 0.97|Accuracy: 0.96|Accuracy: 0.92|\n\n\n*Modified by Kuemmerle-Deschner et al. See online supplementary table 6 for glossary. See table 2 for prerequisite criteria. _CAPS,_ Cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes, _FMF,_ familial Mediterranean fever; _MKD,_ mevalonate kinase deficiency; _PFAPA,_ periodic fever, aphthosis, pharyngitis and adenitis; _PRINTO,_ pediatric rheumatology international trial organization; _TRAPS,_ tumor necrosis factor receptor-associated periodic fever syndrome. From Gattorno M, Hofer M, Federici S, et al. Classification criteria for autoinflammatory recurrent fevers. _Ann Rheum Dis_ . 2019;78:1025-1032 (Table 3, p. 1028).\n\n<!-- p.1281 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p1282_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 57.2** Cutaneous manifestations of inherited autoinflammatory disorders. (A) Annular erythematous plaques studded with pustules in an infant with deficiency of the interleukin-1 receptor antagonist (DIRA); note the similarity to pustular psoriasis. (B) Child with deficiency of interleukin-36 receptor antagonist (DITRA)—widespread psoriasiform plaques whose edges contain pustules. (C) Blau syndrome with numerous, small, pink-tan, flat papules in a generalized distribution in a 1-year-old boy; histologically, a granulomatous infiltrate is seen. (D) STING (STimulator of INterferon Genes)-associated vasculopathy with onset in infancy (SAVI) is characterized by acral violaceous plaques and resorption of digits and loss of nails; patients may be diagnosed as having Aicardi–Goutières syndrome, antiphospholipid antibody syndrome, or vasculitis. (E) Aicardi–Goutières syndrome with pernio-like lesions. (F) Chronic atypical neutrophilic dermatosis with lipodystrophy and elevated temperature (CANDLE) syndrome with deforming arthritis (e.g., swan neck deformity) and thin pink plaques overlying the metacarpophalangeal joints that resemble dermatomyositis. (G) Livedo racemosa in a patient with adenosine deaminase 2 (ADA2) deficiency. (H) Cystic acne and extensive scarring in a pediatric patient with pyogenic arthritis, pyoderma gangrenosum, and acne (PAPA) syndrome. (A, Courtesy Gillian Clarke, MD. B, Courtesy Antonio Torrelo, MD. C,E, Courtesy Julie V. Schaffer, MD. D,F–H, Courtesy Edward Cowen, MD. From Lenormand C, Gattorno M, Lipsker D: Other rheumatologic disorders and autoinflammatory diseases in Bolognia JL, Schaffer JV, Cerroni, eds. _Dermatology_ . 5th ed. Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2025. [Fig. 45.11, p. 750].)\n\n<!-- p.1282 -->\n\nsymptoms, such as fevers or characteristic rashes, is a significant indicator of autoinflammatory disorders (Fig. 57.2). Factors like a positive family history, consanguinity, and an early onset of the disease provide important clues. Advancements in molecular genetics and increased accessibility to genetic testing have expanded our understanding and diagnosis of autoinflammatory disorders across diverse populations and ethnic groups, offering valuable insights into the relationship between genotypes \n\nand phenotypes. Molecular genetic analyses can establish a definitive diagnosis for many cases, but it is crucial to exercise caution in interpreting results, particularly when dealing with variants of uncertain significance (VUS). Given that diseases with similar symptoms may have entirely different molecular mechanisms, or conversely, similar molecular mechanisms may manifest as different symptoms, precise phenotyping is of utmost importance (Table 57.3). \n\n\n#### **TABLE 57.3** The Monogenic Al utoinflammat lory Diseases\n\n|||||||Targeted|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n||Mechanism|Disease|Gene|Inheritance|Clinical Presentation|Therapy|\n||Pyrin activation|FMF|_MEFV_|AR or AD|Fever, pain, (abdominal, chest,<br>joint), rash|IL-1,<br>colchicine|\n|||PAAND|_MEFV_|AD|Fever, myalgia, myositis, rash,<br>abscesses|IL-1,<br>colchicine|\n|||MKD|_MVK_|AR|Fever, pain (abdominal,<br>extremity), vomiting, rash|IL-1|\n|||PAPA|_PSTPIP1_|AD|Pyoderma gangrenosum,<br>arthritis|IL-1, TNF|\n|l     itions||Hz/Hc|_PSTPIP1_|AD|Rash, FTT,<br>hepatosplenomegaly,<br>neutropenia|IL-1, TNF|\n|l    mily cond||PFIT|_WDR1_|AR|Fever, infection, oral<br>inflammation, perianal<br>ulceration|IL-18|\n|l   IL-1 fa|Cryopyrin activation|FCAS|_NLRP3_|AD|Cold urticaria, extremity pain,<br>conjunctivitis, fever|IL-1|\n|l and other||MWS|_NLRP3_|AD|Urticarial rash, extremity pain,<br>hearing loss, conjunctivitis,<br>fever|IL-1|\n|lpathies||NOMID|_NLRP3_|AD|CNS inflammation, urticaria,<br>knee arthropathy, fever|IL-1|\n|lasomo||Majeed’s|_LPIN2_|AR|Osteomyelitis, fevers, rash,<br>dyserythropoietic anemia|IL-1|\n|nflamm|NLRC4 activation|AIFEC|_NLRC4_|AD|Enterocolitis, rash, arthritis,<br>fever|IL-1, IL-18|\n|Il||FCAS/NOMID|_NLRC4_|AD|Cold urticaria, extremity pain,<br>fever, CNS disease|IL-1|\n||NLRP12 activation|FCAS|_NLRP12_|AD|Cold urticaria, extremity pain,<br>fever|TNF, IL-1|\n||NLRP1 activation|NAIAD|_NLRP1_|AD|Ocular, laryngeal, skin<br>dyskeratosis, fever, arthritis|IL-1, TNF|\n||Receptor antagonist<br>deficiency|DIRA|_IL1RN_|AR|Pustular rash, osteomyelitis,<br>periostitis, fever.|IL-1|\n|||DITRA|_IL36RN_|AR|Pustular psoriasis, fever,<br>malaise|TNF, IL-<br>17/12/23?|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.1283 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 57.3** The Monogenic l Autoinflamma ltory Diseases—c l ont'd\n\n|||||||Targeted|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n||Mechanism|Disease|Gene|Inheritance|Clinical Presentation|Therapy|\n||Nucleic acid<br>processing and<br>degradation|Aicardi-<br>Goutières<br>syndrome|_TREX1,_<br>_ADAR1._<br>_RNASEH2A/_<br>_B/C, SAMHD1,_<br>_IFIH1_|<br>AR (AD:<br>_IFIH1_)|Fever, neurological decline,<br>encephalopathy, cerebral<br>calcification, chilblains,<br>autoantibodies|JAK, RTI?|\n|||Monogenic<br>SLE|_DNASE1/2/1L3._<br>_complements_|AR (AD:<br>_DNASE1_)|Autoantibodies, cytopenias,<br>glomerulonephritis, skin rash,<br>oral ulcers, arthritis|JAK?|\n|pathies|Nucleic acid<br>sensing|SMS|_IFIH1, DDX58a_|AD|Calcification of aorta/cardiac<br>valves, osteopenia, acro-<br>osteolysis, dental anomalies|JAK?|\n|rferono||SAVI|_TMEM137_|AD|Chilblain’s rash, small vessel<br>vasculitis, arthritis, ILD|JAK|\n|Type | Inte|Proteasome|CANDLE/<br>PRAAS,<br>PRAID|_PSMB4, PSMA3,_<br>_PSMB8,_<br>_POMP._<br>_PSMG2._<br>_PSMB9,_<br>_PSMB10_|<br>Digenic,<br>AR (AD:<br>POMP)|Fever, joint contractures,<br>annular plaques, eyelid<br>swelling, hepatosplenomegaly<br>lipodystrophy, FTT,<br>developmental delay, anemia|,<br>JAK|\n||IFN signaling|AGS-like|_USP18. ISG15,_<br>_STAT2_|AR|Skin ulcerations, seizures,<br>hydrocephalus, cerebral<br>calcifications, respiratory<br>failure|JAK|\n||Oher|SPENCD|_ACP5_|AR|Skeletal dysplasia, short stature,<br>cerebral calcification,<br>cytopenias, autoantibodies|<br>?|\n||Dysregulation of<br>NFKB signaling|HA20|_TNFAIP3_|AD|Oral, gastrointestinal and<br>genital ulcerations, fever,<br>arthritis, recurrent infection|TNF, IL-1.<br>JAK?|\n|ty||RELA<br>haploinsuf|_RELA_|AD|Oral and gastrointestinal<br>ulcerations, cytopenias,<br>lymphoproliferative disease|TNF|\n|F activi||ORAS|_OTULIN_|AR|Fever, panniculitis, diarrhea,<br>arthritis, FTT|TNF|\n|aberrant TN||LUBAC<br>deficiency|_HOIL1, HOIP_|AR|Fever, recurrent infection, FTT,<br>hepatosplenomegaly,<br>amylopectin-like deposits in<br>muscles|TNF?|\n|and/or|Dysregulation<br>of TNF|Blau|_NOD2_|AD|Granulomatous dermatitis,<br>uveitis, polyarticular arthritis|TNF|\n|NF-kB||TRAPS|_TNFRSF1A_|AD|Episodic fever, abdominal pain,<br>headache, conjunctivitis,<br>painful centrifugal rash|IL-1, TNF|\n|||DADA2|_ADA2_|AR|Systemic vasculitis, fever, rash,<br>stroke, cytopenias.<br>Hypogammaglobulinemia|TNF, HSCT|\n|||CRIA|_RIPK1_|AD|Fever, lymphadenopathy,<br>hepatosplenomegaly|IL-6?|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.1284 -->\n\n##### **TABLE** \n\n#### **The Monogenic Autoinflammatory Diseases—cont'd** \n\n|**57.3**|**g l**<br>Mechanism|**l**<br>Disease|**ly**<br>Gene|**l**<br>Inheritance|Clinical Presentation|Targeted<br>Therapy|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n||Golgi-ER transport|COPA|_COPA_|AD|Arthritis, ILD, diffuse alveolar<br>hemorrhage, autoantibodies|IL-17? JAK?|\n||Intracellular calcium<br>signaling|PLAID|_PLCG2_|AD|Cold urticaria, atopy,<br>granulomatous dermatitis,<br>hypogammaglobulinemia,<br>infection, autoantibodies|?|\n|ms|Intracellular<br>calcium<br>signaling<br>tRNA biogenesis|APLAID|_PLCG2_|AD|Blistering skin lesions,<br>ILD, bronchiolitis, eye<br>inflammation, enterocolitis,<br>immunodeficiency|?|\n|mechanis||SIFD|_TRNT1_|AR|Fever, developmental delay,<br>seizures, microcytic anemia<br>hypogammaglobulinemia|TNF|\n|Other|Lipid metabolism?<br>ER stress?|LACC1<br>deficiency|_LACC1/FAMIN_|AR|Fever, systemic JIA,<br>oligoarticular/polyarticular JIA|?|\n||Cytokine<br>dysregulation|VEO-IBD|_IL-10, IL10RA,_<br>_IL10RB_|AR|Early-onset colitis, FTT|HSCT, IL-1?|\n||Actin assembly|ARPC1B<br>deficiency|_ARPC1B_|AR|Platelet abnormalities,<br>bleeding, recurrent infection,<br>small vessel vasculitis,<br>eczema, arthritis|?|\n||Actin<br>polymerization|CDC42<br>deficiency|_CDC42_|AR|Neurodevelopmental defects,<br>facial dysmorphism cytopenias<br>recurrent infection, fever, rash|,<br>IL-1|\n\n\nPresented is a simplified representation of disease mechanism classification, causative gene, heritability, major clinical manifestations, and typical treatment options for a representative range of monogenic autoinflammatory diseases. \n\n_AD,_ Autosomal dominant; _AIFEC,_ autoinflammation with infantile enterocolitis; _APLAID,_ autoinflammation and PLAID; _AR,_ autosomal recessive; _CANDLE,_ chronic atypical neutrophilic dermatosis with lipodystrophy and elevated temperature; _CNS,_ central nervous system; _CRIA,_ cleavage-resistant RIPK1-induced autoinflammatory syndrome; _DADA2,_ deficiency of adenosine deaminase 2; _DIRA,_ deficiency of IL-1 receptor antagonist; _ER,_ endoplasmic reticulum; _FAMIN,_ fatty acid metabolism-immunity nexus; _FTT,_ failure to thrive; _Haploinsuf.,_ haploinsufficiency; _HSCT,_ hematopoietic stem cell transplant; _HOIL1,_ heme-oxidized IRP2 ubiquitin ligase 1; _HOIP,_ HOIL1-interacting protein; Hz/Hc, hyperzincemia/hypercalprotectinemia; _ILD,_ interstitial lung disease; _JIA,_ juvenile idiopathic arthritis; _LACC1,_ laccase domain-containing 1; _MWS,_ Muckle-Wells syndrome; _NOMID,_ neonatal-onset multisystem inflammatory disease; _ORAS,_ OTULINrelated autoinflammatory syndrome; _PAAND,_ pyrin-associated autoinflammation with neutrophilic dermatosis; _PAPA,_ pyogenic arthritis, pyoderma gangrenosum and acne; _PFIT,_ periodic fever, immunodeficiency, and thrombocytopenia; _PLAID,_ PLCG2-associated antibody deficiency and immune dysregulation; _PLCG2,_ phospholipase C gamma 2; _PRAAS,_ proteasome-associated autoinflammatory syndrome; _PRAID,_ POMP-related autoinflammation and immune dysregulation disease; _RTI,_ reverse-transcriptase inhibitor; _SAVI,_ STING-associated vasculopathy of infancy; _SIFD,_ sideroblastic anemia with B-cell immunodeficiency, periodic fevers, and developmental delay; _SLE,_ systemic lupus erythematosus; _SMS,_ Singleton-Merten syndrome; _SPENCD,_ spondyloenchondrodysplasia; _tRNA,_ transfer ribonucleic acid; _VEO-IBD,_ very early onset inflammatory bowel disease. \n\nFrom Nigrovic PA, Lee PY, Hoffman HM. Monogenic autoinflammatory disorders: conceptual overview, phenotype, and clinical approach. _J Allergy Clin Immunol_ . 2020;146(5):925–937, Fig. 3. \n\nAlso from Verbsky JW. Hereditary periodic fever syndromes and other systemic inflammatory diseases. In: Kliegman RM, St. Geme JW III, eds _Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics_ . 22nd ed. Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2025, (Table 204.2, p. 1519-1520). \n\n## **IL-1 Related Autoinflammatory Diseases and Inflammasomopathies** \n\nInflammasomes are macromolecular structures within the cytoplasm of the cell sensing molecular motifs associated with danger from a pathogen or from tissue damage, known as Pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs) and damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs) respectively (Fig. 57.1 and Fig. 57.3). These are sensed by a variety of cellular proteins, but for the inflammasome _NLRP3_ \n\n(NOD like receptor family, pyrin domain-containing 3) is the molecular sensor which assembles the inflammasome resulting ultimately in activation of caspase-1. Caspase-1, in turn, cleaves the proinflammatory cytokines interleukin (IL)-1β and IL-18 into their active forms. Additionally, caspase-1 activates gasdermin D resulting in a form of cell death called pyroptosis. The end result is the release of IL-1β and IL-18 into the extracellular space, where they are detected by various immune cells, subsequently promoting an inflammatory response. Inflammasomopathies can be\n\n<!-- p.1285 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p1286_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 57.3** Mechanism of the NLRP3 inflammasome pathway. NLRP3 inflammasomes are formed by priming and activation signaling. In the priming signaling, several pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs) or danger-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs) bind to the pattern-recognition receptors (PRRs) such as Toll-like receptors and activate the NF-κB signaling pathway, which leads to the upregulation of NLRP3, pro-interleukin (IL)-1β, and pro-IL-18. In the activation signaling, various PAMPs, DAMPs, or intracellular changes induce the formation of the NLRP3 inflammasome composed of NLRP3 as a PRR, pro-caspase-1, and adapter proteins such as the apoptosis-associated speck-like protein containing a caspase recruitment domain that connects NLRP3 and pro-caspase-1. Then, the inflammasome complex activates pro-caspase-1. The activated caspase-1 cleaves the pro-IL-1β and pro-IL-18 into IL-1β and IL-18, which are biologically active forms. _ASC,_ Apoptosis-associated speck-like protein containing a caspase recruitment domain; _DAMP,_ danger-associated molecular pattern; _IL,_ interleukin; _NF-_ κB, nuclear factor kappa B; _NLRP3,_ NOD-like receptor family pyrin domain containing 3; _TLR,_ Toll-like receptor.  (From Shimizu T, Nakamura H, Kawakami A. Role of the innate immunity signaling pathway in the pathogenesis of Sjogren’s syndrome. _Int J Mol Sci_ . 2021;22:3090 [Fig. 3].) \n\nbroadly categorized as conditions primarily driven by either IL-1β or IL-18. Interleukin-1β is central to many autoinflammatory disorders, particularly those associated with fevers. \n\n### **Familial Mediterranean Fever** \n\nFamilial Mediterranean Fever (FMF) is a prototypic autoinflammatory disease and the most prevalent monogenic autoinflammatory disorder in the world. It is classically \n\ninherited in an autosomal recessive manner, although patients with FMF have been described with one variant and thus appear to have a gene dosage affect. FMF is notably prevalent among populations with Eastern European origin, including Jews, Turks, Armenians, and Levantine Arabs. The variants causing FMF are present at a very high frequency in the aforementioned populations, leading to a higher incidence of the disease in regions like the Mediterranean basin and the Middle East. The disease may affect as many as 1 in 500 individuals in these areas,\n\n<!-- p.1286 -->\n\nsuggesting some selective advantage in that geographic area. There is evidence that _MEFV_ variants associated with FMF could have a selective advantage in survival from the bubonic plague. In one study, mononuclear cells from FMF patients and individuals who are heterozygous carriers released higher levels of IL-1β in response to _Yersinia pestis_ infection when tested _ex vivo_ . \n\nThe _MEFV_ (MEditerranean FeVer) gene encodes a protein product named pyrin (Latin for fever) or marenostrin (Latin for sea). Pyrin is an essential protein in the NLRP3 inflammasome complex. Gain-of-function variant of _MEFV_ causes excess inflammation via caspase-1 activation and IL-1β production. Interleukin-1 is responsible for both the clinical symptoms and the laboratory markers indicating episodes of inflammation and their sequelae in FMF. \n\nThere are over 390 different _MEFV_ variants. An international guideline regarding the genetic diagnosis of hereditary recurrent fevers including FMF revised in 2020 to integrate the new discoveries and changes in classification of variants of unknown significance. An example is the p.Glu148Gln variant in _MEFV_ , which is a common gene variant that is not always associated with the disease phenotype but may be associated with milder clinical presentations that deviate from the typical manifestations of FMF. Similar variants may not consistently follow patterns of inheritance within affected families. Some of these variants might act as risk factors, or their clinical impact may be influenced by additional genetic factors (e.g., other _MEFV_ variants) or environmental factors. However, it is essential to understand the genetic variants because some are more classically associated with severe disease and its long term complications, such as amyloidosis. \n\nFamilial mediterranean fever (FMF) is characterized by episodes of fever lasting 1 to 3 days, accompanied by sterile peritonitis and/or pleural inflammation, arthritis, and rarely an erysipelas-like rash. Typically, the first attack occurs in childhood, often before the age of 20, and the timing between attacks can vary, _with no_ periodicity. During episodes, young children may primarily experience fever, which is the most common symptom, whereas abdominal pain becomes more prominent as they grow older. Chest pain if due to pleural inflammation is usually unilateral, but if it’s due to pericarditis, it tends to be retrosternal and positional. The arthritis affects the larger joints of the lower extremities, such as knees and ankles. Although skin rash is generally not observed during FMF attacks, some individuals with severe variants may develop spontaneously resolving erysipelas-like erythema, often localized around the ankle joints. Subclinical inflammation can also be part of the FMF phenotype and may manifest as nonspecific symptoms like myalgia and fatigue. Elevated levels of acute phase reactants (especially serum amyloid A or SAA protein) serve as a key indicator of subclinical inflammation in FMF. If not treated, these episodes and the ongoing inflammation can occasionally lead to systemic amyloidosis. Patients with FMF may have associated comorbidities including ankylosing \n\nspondylitis (sacroiliitis), Behçet disease, IgA vasculitis, juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA), polyarthritis nodosa (PAN) and multiple sclerosis. \n\nA number of diagnostic criteria have been proposed for FMF; in the Eurofever/PRINTO (Pediatric Rheumatology INternational Trials Organization) endorsed criteria for a patient with a pathogenic variant for FMF (Table 57.1). \n\nIf the _MEFV_ genotype is not confirmatory, a patient should have two out of four clinical features (Table 57.2). It is also recommended to perform a comprehensive panel of tests for other autoinflammatory syndromes, particularly when the patient’s ethnic background does not align with Eastern Mediterranean origins. \n\nA confirmatory or consistent genotype for FMF is defined as the presence of biallelic (likely) pathogenic variants in _MEFV_ (Table 57.4). In addition, the presence of one (likely) pathogenic and 1 rare/novel VUS in _MEFV_ is considered a consistent genotype when these variants are in _trans_ . Parental _MEFV_ testing is recommended to solve the complex allele issue ( _cis_ or _trans_ ) especially if the variants are in different exons. Diagnosis still relies on clinical judgement in this case. The presence of only one (likely) pathogenic variant or two rare VUS in _MEFV_ gene are considered inconclusive genotypes. It is important to highlight that the presence of one pathogenic _MEFV_ variant could lead to an FMF (Table 57.4). \n\n**Colchicine** is the standard therapy for individuals with FMF. Its primary role is to reduce both the clinical symptoms and laboratory markers of inflammation and also preventing the development of AA amyloidosis. Colchicine should be started as soon as the clinical diagnosis of FMF is made. The standard starting dose of colchicine varies based on age: for children under 5 years of age, the recommended starting dose is 0.5 mg per day; children aged 5 to 10 years may start with a dose of 0.5 to 1 mg per day; children over 10 years of age and adults may begin with a dose of 1 to 1.5 mg per day. The maximum recommended colchicine dose for the treatment of FMF, depending on age and to a lesser extent body weight, is in the range of 1 to 3 mg per day. Although the conventional recommendation suggests twice-daily usage, one randomized controlled trial has demonstrated that using colchicine with either a once-daily or twice-daily dosage provides comparable clinical and laboratory improvements. Patients should be regularly monitored for a duration \n\n\n#### **TABLE 57.4** Variants to Be Te for FMF sted in theMEFV Gene\n\n|Classification|_MEFV_Gene Variant|\n|---|---|\n|Most common pathogen<br>variants|M694V, M694I, M680I,<br>V726A, R761H, A744S,<br>I692del, E167D, T267I|\n|Most common variants of<br>unknown significance|E148Q, P369S, F479L, I591T,<br>K695R|\n|Benign variants|R202Q, R408Q|\n\n<!-- p.1287 -->\n\nof 3 to 6 months to assess the therapeutic effect of colchicine on the frequency and severity of attacks, and for potential side effects, mainly gastrointestinal symptoms. Colchicine intolerance is common, often characterized by mild gastrointestinal symptoms (diarrhea and nausea). \n\nUp to 5% of patients do not respond to colchicine treatment, leading to a risk of persistent inflammation and its associated complications. This lack of response could be attributed to genetic and/or environmental factors that influence disease severity and/or the colchicine bioavailability. In cases where a patient is already receiving the highest tolerable colchicine dose, resistance to colchicine is defined as the presence of ongoing disease activity. This is indicated by either recurrent clinical attacks (averaging one or more attacks per month over a 3-month period) or persistently elevated levels of CRP (C-reactive protein) or SAA (serum amyloid A) between attacks (depending on the available local testing), in the absence of any other plausible explanation. Response to treatment should be followed with a validated disease activity tool as the Autoinflammatory Disease Activity Index (AIDAI) which can be used for all monogenic diseases associated with periodic fevers. Because FMF is an inflammasomopathy and IL-1 plays an important role in the pathogenesis, anti-IL-1 treatment can be used in treatment of colchicine-resistant patients. **Anakinra treatment** is effective and safe in colchicine-resistant FMF patients in one randomized–controlled study. In a study evaluating the long-term safety and efficacy data of canakinumab (monoclonal antibody against IL-1β) in colchicine-resistant FMF patients, patients also confirmed a good control of disease activity and the median CRP levels remained in the normal range. \n\nIf left untreated, FMF can lead to chronic sequelae of inflammation. These may include conditions such as anemia, growth impairment in children, reduced bone density, and a decrease in overall quality of life. However, amyloidosis stands out as the most severe complication. \n\n### **Pyrin Associated Autoinflammation With Neutrophilic Dermatosis (PAAND)** \n\nA rare dominant autoinflammatory disease characterized by a type of skin inflammation involving neutrophils is associated with variants found in exon 2 of _MEFV_ and has been termed PAAND, which stands for Pyrin Associated Autoinflammation with <u>Neutrophilic Dermatosis. Individuals</u> affected by PAAND exhibit recurring episodes of fever, along with symptoms such as joint pain and neutrophilic dermatosis that typically begin early in life. Neutrophilic dermatosis encompasses various skin conditions, including pustular acne, suppurative hidradenitis, pyoderma gangrenosum, and neutrophilic panniculitis. PAAND may also involve gastrointestinal and cardiac manifestations, which can resemble the symptoms seen in another condition called **otulipenia** . \n\nGiven the molecular pathogenesis similarities to FMF, the treatment approach for the condition typically involves \n\nthe use of colchicine in combination with low-dose prednisolone. Individuals experiencing systemic and/or gastrointestinal inflammation may benefit from medications like anakinra and canakinumab. In cases where cutaneous and/or joint symptoms are prominent, adalimumab can be a treatment option. Isotretinoin may be used as part of the therapeutic regimen for the cutaneous component. \n\n### **Pyogenic Arthritis With Pyoderma Gangrenosum and Acne (PAPA) Syndrome** \n\nPyogenic arthritis with pyoderma gangrenosum and acne (PAPA) syndrome is an autoinflammatory disorder that primarily affects the skin and joints (Fig. 57.2H). PAPA syndrome caused by genetic variants in _PSTPIP1_ (prolineserine-threonine phosphatase interacting protein-1) gene, also known as CD2-binding protein 1 (CD2BP1). PSTPIP1 interacts with a protein tyrosine phosphatase known as PTP-PEST, which is rich in proline (P), glutamic acid (E), serine (S), and threonine (T) residues. \n\nClinical manifestations of PAPA syndrome include recurrent arthritis, synovial fluid that is sterile but purulent, and skin manifestations such as pyoderma gangrenosum (characterized by large, open purulent lesions) and severe cystic acne. PSTPIP1 interacts with pyrin indicating that significant, though not exclusive, involvement of the inflammasome and IL-1β in its pathogenesis. Blockade of IL-1β has shown effectiveness in the treatment of PAPA patients. \n\n### **Cryopyrin-Associated Periodic Syndromes (CAPS)** \n\nCryopyrin associated periodic syndrome (CAPS) is a systemic inflammatory disorder that can affect multiple organs within the body due to genetic variants in _NLRP3_ (also known as _CIAS1_ ) which encodes a protein named cryopyrin. Disease causing variants within _NLRP3_ are autosomal dominantly inherited or _de novo_ gainof-function variants, although somatic variants or genetic mosaicism may occur. Cryopyrin serves as the nucleation point for an intracellular multi-molecular assembly referred to as the NLRP3 inflammasome (Fig. 57.1). This complex encompasses specific adaptor proteins, including ASC (apoptosis-associated speck-like protein containing a caspase recruitment domain) and various chaperone proteins, and can be modulated by other interacting proteins such as PSTPIP1 or pyrin. Once assembled the complex activates Caspase-1 which cleaves pro-IL-1β and pro-IL-18 into their biologically active forms, namely IL-1β and IL-18. Physiological activation of the NLRP3 inflammasome can be triggered by a diverse array of stimuli, including both pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs) and danger-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs). Disease causing variants result in ligand-independent constitutive activation of the NLRP3 inflammasome and IL-1β release with some genotypicphenotypic association.\n\n<!-- p.1288 -->\n\nThe disease can result in daily chronic symptoms or can be episodic and triggered by the environment; symptoms can be variable. Onset of CAPS symptoms usually occurs early in infancy as a rash soon after birth, but the median age of disease onset is typically around 0.8 years, with a range of 0.1 to 5 years. However, in patients with somatic variants, a late-onset variant of the condition has been observed, with a median age of onset at 50 years. Cryopyrin associated periodic syndrome appears to be more prevalent among individuals of Caucasian descent; no significant gender differences have been noted. Noteworthy factors in a patient’s medical history that may raise suspicion of CAPS include specific triggers (cold exposure), the presence of characteristic disease symptoms, or a family history of early hearing loss or renal transplants. \n\nCryopyrin associated periodic syndrome (CAPS) was initially coined as an umbrella term encompassing three distinct disorders known as familial cold-induced autoinflammatory syndrome (FCAS), Muckle-Wells syndrome (MWS), and neonatal onset multisystem inflammatory disease (NOMID). However, a consensus proposal aimed at establishing a new classification system for monogenic autoinflammatory disorders suggested adopting the term **NLRP3-associated autoinflammatory diseases** to refer to the broader spectrum of conditions within CAPS. These disorders are categorized by severity, ranging from mild to severe: **FCAS** is characterized by recurrent fevers triggered by exposure to cold, accompanied by a rash resembling urticaria and constitutional symptoms; **MWS** typically manifests as a chronic pattern rather than recurring episodes featuring fevers, rash, sensorineural hearing loss, eye inflammation, and arthritis, with symptoms usually unrelated to cold exposure; **NOMID** (or chronic infantile neurological cutaneous articular **[CINCA]** syndrome) presents with fever, urticaria, overgrowth of the long bones’ epiphyses, and chronic aseptic meningitis. In severe cases of CINCA/ NOMID, patients may develop hypertrophic arthropathy with contractures and bone deformities. \n\nThese syndromes exist along a spectrum and present with fever, central nervous system (CNS) and ocular inflammation, rash, arthritis, and bony inflammation. Symptoms are often precipitated by cold exposure; however, up to half of patients experience daily symptoms. Although there is some overlap between the clinical symptoms of FMF and CAPS, there is clearly a difference as well, with greater CNS, skin, and bone disease in CAPS. Classification is noted in Tables 57.1 and 57.2. \n\nThe Infevers database, which serves as a comprehensive repository for sequence variants identified in different autoinflammatory disorders, lists more than 260 variants in _NLRP3_ . If one clearly pathogenic variant is identified, it confirms the diagnosis of CAPS; in cases where a variant of uncertain significance is found or no variants are identified, the diagnosis may need to rely on clinical judgement and diagnostic criteria. In the latter situation, parental testing and familial segregation analysis can be instrumental in understanding its clinical relevance. Moreover, it is \n\nwell-established that a significant proportion of patients with CAPS may exhibit mosaicism due to somatic variants; routine genetic analysis may fail to detect the variant. Therefore, when there is a strong clinical suspicion of CAPS, it becomes imperative to specifically search for mosaicism within the CAPS-associated gene. Somatic variants can be missed using conventional methods such as Sanger sequencing due to the low frequency of the variant allele in peripheral blood. Next generation sequencing (NGS)-based methods with greater depth of coverage are required to detect somatic variants. \n\nIn the assessment of inflammatory flares and symptomfree intervals, primary laboratory tests commonly include the evaluation of inflammatory markers such as C-reactive protein (CRP), erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR), serum amyloid A (SAA), and a complete blood count. Common findings in MWS and NOMID include leukocytosis, neutrophilia, thrombocytosis, and anemia. In FCAS, these abnormalities are seen during a flare but _can be normal_ when asymptomatic. In cases of inflammatory flares, additional diagnostic clues can be noted from the analysis of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF); elevated neopterin levels and increased protein content in the CSF can be observed during these periods, providing valuable insights into the presence and severity of central nervous system involvement in the inflammatory process. It is essential to consider other disorders that can present with recurrent systemic inflammation. This includes a thorough evaluation for conditions such as immunodeficiencies, infections, autoimmune diseases, and malignancies. If these first steps support the suspicion of CAPS, musculoskeletal, neurological, and ophthalmological examination is suggested. In patients with severe musculoskeletal involvement, X-ray and bone magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) should be performed. Molecular diagnosis should be pursued when the clinical phenotype and laboratory results strongly suggests the presence of CAPS. \n\nBecause IL-1 has a central role in CAPS pathogenesis, **anti-IL-1 treatment** is recommended for the whole CAPS spectrum. Three anti-IL-1 treatments (anakinra, canakinumab, and rilonacept) are available. Some patients benefit from nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) and corticosteroids, mostly as on-demand symptom relief, however, there is no evidence for the efficacy of disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs or biological agents other than IL-1 blockers. The CNS penetrance of anakinra seems to be excellent; therefore this might be the treatment of choice in cases with aseptic meningitis. Anakinra has a very significant impact in even the most severe cryopyrinopathy, NOMID/CINCA, with dramatic effects on not only the rash and acute-phase proteins, but also on the aseptic meningitis and cochlear inflammation, which often lead to severe disability. Although anti-IL-1 treatment is recommended as a therapeutic approach for all phenotypes within the CAPS spectrum and is known to be effective across the entire range of CAPS severity, it remains crucial to differentiate among the\n\n<!-- p.1289 -->\n\nsubphenotypes, especially in cases of moderate to severe CAPS phenotypes. This distinction is important because more aggressive and intensive treatment strategies are often required to achieve remission and prevent organ damage in severe phenotypes. \n\nAdditional monitoring includes serial physical examinations, measurements of weight and height, audiology and ophthalmological exams, radiographs and MRIs, as well as musculoskeletal, neurological, and laboratory examinations, such as blood count, liver and muscle enzymes, renal function, urine analysis, CSF measurements, and determination of SAA, ESR, and CRP levels to detect ongoing inflammation. Predicting the course of CAPS is a challenge. However, it’s crucial to note that patients with specific variants like T348M or D303N are at a heightened risk of developing severe neurological complications and neurosensory hearing loss. These patients should be monitored closely, and treatment should be discussed early, to avoid long-term complications. Monitoring disease activity in CAPS is essential for effective management. The AIDAI is a valuable tool for systematically assessing daily disease symptoms in CAPS patients. This validated instrument is particularly useful for outpatients, as it enables them to selfassess CAPS disease activity at home. The data collected through AIDAI assessments can provide treating physicians with valuable insights into the patient’s condition, helping to differentiate between periods of inactive or active disease and informing decisions about the need for treatment adjustments. In addition to monitoring disease activity, it’s equally important to assess any disease-related organ damage. For this purpose, the Autoinflammatory Disease Damage Index (ADDI) is a reliable instrument. It allows healthcare providers to assess structural damage in individual CAPS patients, facilitating ongoing monitoring of the disease’s impact on various organs. \n\n### **NLRP12-Associated Autoinflammatory Disease/Guadeloupe Variant Periodic Fever Syndrome (FCAS2)** \n\nRecurrent fevers associated with _NLRP12_ variants involving twins from Guadeloupe who experienced symptoms such as arthralgia, periodic fever, and cold-induced urticaria and was named the Guadeloupe Variant Periodic Fever Syndrome, also known as FCAS2 (Familial Cold Autoinflammatory Syndrome 2). Their condition spontaneously improved during adolescence. Several additional cases have emerged, demonstrating significant clinical diversity, including mild immunodeficiency. Dominantly inherited nonsense and splice-site variants in _NLRP12_ have been identified. Patients with this syndrome typically present with week-long episodic fevers that are triggered by exposure to cold temperatures. These fever episodes are often accompanied by symptoms such as arthralgia, myalgia, and other constitutional symptoms. In some cases, affected individuals may also experience sensorineural hearing loss. \n\nDespite clinical similarities to FCAS and MWS, sequence analysis of _NLRP3_ was negative and variants in _NLRP12_ were found. NLRP12 shares similarities with NLRP3, is expressed in myelomonocytic cells, and can activate the inflammasome resulting in IL-1β and IL-18 activation. Case reports have shown efficacy of anakinra. \n\n### **Deficiency of Interleukin-1 Receptor Antagonist (DIRA)** \n\nVariants in _IL1RN_ responsible for encoding the IL-1 receptor antagonist protein (IL-1Ra), result in a syndrome characterized by several key features, including pustular skin rashes, inflammation and excessive growth of bones (including periosteal elevation, osteopenia, epiphyseal ballooning, and lytic lesions), as well as elevated levels of ESR (erythrocyte sedimentation rate) and CRP (Fig. 57.1A). Fever _does not_ appear to be a prominent symptom in this syndrome. Recessively inherited variants including frameshift and nonsense variants, or chromosomal deletions, lead to the loss of IL-1Ra protein. IL-1Ra is a naturally occurring inhibitor of IL-1β, competitively blocking IL-1β activity by binding to the IL-1 receptor. Consequently, treatment with recombinant IL-1Ra has proven effective in alleviating symptoms. It is worth noting that IL-1Ra prevents the binding of both IL-1β and IL-1α, and some of the differences in clinical presentation between IL-1Ra deficiency and other inflammasomopathies may be related to the activity of IL-1α. \n\n### **Majeed Syndrome** \n\nMajeed syndrome is an autoinflammatory disorder consisting of the classic triad of severe early onset noninfectious osteomyelitis, microcytic congenital dyserythropoietic anemia and neutrophilic dermatosis. It is a rare recessive condition; most published cases demonstrated consanguinity. Symptoms typically present prior to 2 years of age and are characterized by frequent episodes of bone pain, usually worse at night with swelling and/or warmth, and fever. Because the epiphyses are affected this can mimic arthritis. Imaging reveals osteolytic lesions with surrounding sclerosis that are most commonly located along the growth plates of the long bones but may occur elsewhere; spinal involvement has not been reported. Technicium<sup>99</sup> bone scan or MRI reveals signal predominantly in the metaphyseal regions, but also in the epiphyseal regions of the long bones. Histopathology demonstrates sterile osteomyelitis similar to what is seen in nonsyndromic chronic nonbacterial osteomyelitis. The congenital dyserythropoietic anemia of Majeed syndrome have microcytic anemia, and often require red blood cell transfusion. The neutrophilic dermatosis is the least common feature of Majeed. Hepatosplenomegaly and poor growth are common, as is a marked elevation of acute phase reactants including erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein.\n\n<!-- p.1290 -->\n\nMajeed syndrome is caused by homozygous or compound heterozygous variants in _LPIN2_ ; missense, frameshift, and spice site variants have been reported. _LPIN2_ encodes the protein Lipin-2, a member of the LIPIN family of proteins that acts as a phosphatidate phosphatase enables the conversion of phosphatidic acid to diacylglycerol; how this leads to autoinflammation is unclear. IL-1 is likely important in the pathophysiology because treatment with IL-1 blocking agents result in marked clinical improvement. Studies have suggested that Lipin-2 is a negative regulator of the NLRP3 inflammasome by regulating MAPK activation, which is important for synthesis of pro-IL-1β during NLRP3 inflammasome priming, and altering the activation of the P2X7 receptor, ASC oligomerization and caspase-1 processing. \n\nMany treatments have been tried in Majeed syndrome including NSAIDs, corticosteroids, colchicine, methotrexate, and pamidronate either alone or in combination with variable success. Tumor necrosis factor inhibitors have not been effective but treatment with IL-1 inhibitors (e.g., anakinra, canakinumab) is reported to cause a marked sustained improvement of bone inflammation, inflammatory markers and anemia. \n\n## **IL-18 Related Autoinflammatory Diseases** \n\nThe biology of IL-18 closely parallels that of IL-1β because it is initially produced as a proprotein and later cleaved by the inflammasome into its active form. However, the regulation of IL-18’s transcription and translation differs from that of IL-1β. Interleukin-18 is consistently expressed in mononuclear cells and is also found in the gut epithelium. When combined with IL-12, IL-18 stimulates interferongamma (IFN-γ) responses in T cells and natural killer cells. Unlike IL-1β, IL-18 may not require additional priming signals beyond the inflammasome for activation. IL-18 binding protein (IL-18BP) serves as the natural antagonist for IL-18. It accomplishes this by binding to IL-18 molecules themselves, preventing them from binding to their receptors. Interferon-γ induces the production of IL-18BP, with a feedback mechanism to regulate IL-18 activity. \n\nDiseases associated with excessively high levels of IL-18 have revealed common characteristics involving a combination of **hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis/macrophage activation syndrome (HLH/MAS)** and gastrointestinal inflammation. This connection is not surprising, given the strong link between IL-18 and IFN-γ, which is well-known in the context of HLH. The intestinal epithelium is a major source of IL-18, explaining its involvement in these conditions. \n\n## **Autoinflammation With Infantile Enterocolitis (AIFEC)/NLRC4-AID** \n\nHeterozygous gain-of-function variants in _NLRC4_ (NODlike family caspase activation and recruitment domaincontaining protein-4) result in a syndrome of HLH/MAS, severe intestinal inflammation, skin rash, and fever. Patients \n\nwith these gain-of-function variants demonstrate high serum levels of IL-18. The Infevers database listed more than 30 variants of _NLRC4_ in September 2023. Although 75% are associated with autosomal dominant transmission, the remaining cases are caused by de novo variants. _NLRC4_ variants result in ligand-independent, spontaneous activation of caspase-1 in cells transduced with mutant protein. _Two different phenotypes have been described:_ \n\n- **A severe phenotype (NLRC4-AID phenotype 1)** with neonatal onset and a high mortality rate (40%), presenting with high fevers, macrophage activation syndrome (MAS), severe enterocolitis, and a nonspecific skin eruption. \n\n- **A mild phenotype (NLRC4-AID phenotype 2)** with onset in childhood, a cutaneous tropism with urticaria and erythematous nodules, arthromyalgia, and conjunctivitis. This has not been associated with vital organ involvement, growth restriction, or mortality. \n\nIn both phenotypes, IL-18 is always markedly elevated when assayed both during and in-between episodes. Treatment has not been well established. Patients with a mild phenotype may find relief with NSAIDs, and in some cases, no treatment may be necessary. For individuals experiencing skin manifestations and symptoms resembling NOMID, anakinra can be a suitable treatment option. Treatment of MAS episodes is with corticosteroids, cyclosporine, and IVIg in patients. Anakinra may also help reduce the severity and frequency of MAS episodes. During an MAS episode, IL-1 blockade is considered the primary treatment; there is evidence suggesting the efficacy of IFNγ blockade and possibly IL-18 blockade as well. \n\n## **Mevalonate Kinase Deficiency/ Hyperimmunoglobulinemia D and Periodic Fever Syndrome** \n\nThe enzyme mevalonate kinase (MVK) is widely expressed throughout the body and has a crucial role in a key step of the biosynthetic pathway for both cholesterol and nonsterol isoprenes. MVK converts mevalonic acid into 5-phosphomevalonic acid. When there are severe loss-of-function variants in both copies of _MVK_ , it can lead to a rare metabolic disorder known as mevalonic aciduria. **Mevalonic aciduria** is characterized by a range of clinical symptoms, including recurrent fever, intellectual disability, developmental abnormalities, and early death. Although it is primarily considered a metabolic disease due to the disruption in metabolic pathways, one notable aspect of mevalonic aciduria is its propensity to trigger increased production of the proinflammatory cytokine IL-1. Increased IL-1 production leads to inflammation, further complicating the clinical picture. Consequently, mevalonic aciduria represents a unique condition that involves _both_ metabolic dysfunction and a significant inflammatory component. \n\nThe range of diseases associated with variants in the mevalonate kinase _(MVK)_ gene consist of two distinct\n\n<!-- p.1291 -->\n\nphenotypes: hyperimmunoglobulinemia D and periodic fever syndrome (HIDS) and mevalonic aciduria. In cases of mevalonic aciduria, variants in MVK are often concentrated in specific locations crucial for the enzyme’s catalytic activity. This results in a more severe form of the disorder. Conversely, variants linked to HIDS are spread more broadly across the protein sequence of _MVK_ , affecting various regions. \n\nThe onset is very early in life, with clinical manifestations of fever episodes, abdominal pain, vomiting, diarrhea, lymphadenopathy, and rashes lasting 3 to 6 days. Attacks are provoked by specific triggers such as immunizations, surgery, trauma, and infections. Inflammatory bowel disease has also been reported. Classification criteria for HIDS/ MKD are noted in Tables 57.1 and 57.2. \n\nMKD/HIDS is the only autoinflammatory disease in which a laboratory test other than genetic screening is useful. IgD levels are increased both during attacks and under basal condition, and IgA levels have also been reported to be increased in some cases. Symptoms can occur in children some years _before_ the elevation of serum IgD concentration is found; IgD concentrations do not correlate with disease activity or severity. Additionally, some individuals with variants have _normal_ IgD concentrations highlighting the complexity of its clinical presentation and the fact that the absence of elevated IgD levels does not rule out the possibility of the disease. In 93% of patients during attacks, there is an elevated excretion of mevalonic acid in the urine. This increase in urinary mevalonic acid levels can serve as a valuable diagnostic tool and biomarker for the disease. \n\nNSAIDs can be effective in HIDS/MKD, and corticosteroids have been tried for HIDS attacks. In the Eurofever Registry, NSAIDs were mostly used as on-demand therapy to treat symptomatic relief during attacks. High-dose corticosteroids administered at the onset of an attack can reduce the severity and duration of febrile episodes. Colchicine is not an effective therapy in patients with HIDS/MKD. Because of the increased IL-1β secretion in patients with HIDS/MKD, IL-1 blockade has emerged as a therapeutic option. A small prospective study found a 50% reduction in the severity and duration of febrile episodes in patients with HIDS/MKD using on demand therapy with anakinra when administered in the first 24 hours of an episode. For patients with HIDS/MKD who experience frequent episodes or ongoing subclinical inflammation between episodes, continuous therapy with IL-1-blocking agents or anti-TNF drugs is recommended. These treatment approaches help manage the condition and reduce the risk of long-term complications. Response rates to treatment with anakinra, etanercept, and canakinumab are reported as 90%, 50%, and 100%, respectively. In cases where anti-IL1 and anti-TNF therapies do not yield significant improvement, tocilizumab may be considered as an alternative treatment option. In very severe cases of HIDS/MKD, hematopoietic stem cell transplantation may be indicated. \n\n# **Unfolded Protein Responses/Endoplasmic Reticulum Stress Syndromes** \n\n## **Tumor Necrosis Factor Receptor-Associated Periodic Fever Syndrome (TRAPS)** \n\nDominantly inherited heterozygous variants in the TNF receptor superfamily member 1A _(TNFRSF1A)_ gene, located on chromosome 12, encoding the TNF receptor 1 (TNFR1, also known as TNFRSF1A, p55, and CD120a), are responsible for causing a condition known as tumor necrosis factor receptor-associated periodic fever syndrome (TRAPS). This condition is characterized by recurrent fever episodes, myalgia (muscle pain), skin rashes, as well as elevated white blood cell counts and sedimentation rates. Tumor necrosis factor receptor-associated periodic fever syndrome (TRAPS) was described in a large Irish family. This led to the name **familial Hibernian fever** , as Hibernia is the Latin word for Ireland. Tumor necrosis factor receptorassociated periodic fever syndrome (TRAPS) is recognized as one of the more common inherited recurrent periodic fever syndromes, with an estimated prevalence of 1 case per 1 million individuals worldwide. Although it is most frequently reported in Caucasian and Asian populations, it has since been identified in various ethnic groups across the world, supporting the notion of dominant inheritance for this condition. \n\nTNFR1 belongs to a superfamily of TNF receptors which interact with one or more cytokines of the TNF family. In addition to its role in cell death regulation, ligand binding to these receptors leads to activation of the NF-κB inflammatory pathway or caspase-mediated signaling. _TNFRSF1_ is comprised of 10 coding exons. More than 180 variants of _TNFRSF1_ are listed in the Infevers database. The most common pathogenic variant is p.Thr79Met (T79M, also known as T50M), and the most known low-penetrance variants are p.Arg121Glu (R121Q, also known as R92Q) and p.Pro75Leu (P75L, also known as P46L). The P46L variant is exclusively found in people of African ancestry, whereas R92Q is found primarily in Caucasian populations. Patients who have R92Q are less likely to have a positive family history for periodic fever disorder, present with later onset milder manifestations and have a higher rate of spontaneous resolution than patients with structural variants. There were two reports of somatic variants in patients with adult-onset TRAPS. \n\nPatients experience recurrent and often prolonged fevers occurring every 4 to 6 weeks and lasting from 5 days to 3 weeks that can be accompanied by severe abdominal pain, pleurisy, arthritis, a migratory skin rash with underlying fasciitis, and/or periorbital edema; typically the CNS is spared. Serosal inflammation secondary to TRAPS inflammatory attacks can cause recurrent episodes of pericarditis, peritonitis, pleurisy, and testicular inflammation. Emotional and physical stress, minor infections, trauma, hormonal changes, fatigue, or vaccinations may trigger episodes but most of the\n\n<!-- p.1292 -->\n\ntime the trigger is unknown. During periods of active disease, symptoms are consistently accompanied by elevated levels of acute phase reactants including CRP, ESR, SAA, fibrinogen. Additionally, neutrophil leukocytosis, thrombocytosis, and normochromic anemia (secondary to chronic inflammation) can be observed. Between attacks, acute phase reactants can be elevated, but at lower levels than during a flare. \n\nThe Eurofever classification criteria for TRAPS are noted in Tables 57.1 and 57.2. \n\nAccording to the guideline for genetic diagnosis of hereditary recurrent fevers, if one clearly pathogenic variant is identified, it confirms the clinical diagnosis of TRAPS, but if one variant of uncertain significance is identified, the diagnosis relies on clinical judgement or criteria. Parental testing/familial segregation may help understanding the clinical significance of this variant. If no pathogenic variant is identified, the diagnosis relies on clinical judgement or criteria because mosaicism may exist. \n\nPatients with low-penetrance variants (R121Q, P75L, D41E, V124M, R133Q) are mostly managed with NSAIDs and do not generally require therapy with biological agents. High doses of corticosteroids are effective at reducing inflammation. However, for individuals with chronic symptoms, prolonged and escalating corticosteroid doses may be necessary to maintain control. An alternative approach involves the use of biological treatments that specifically target immune mediators like TNF-α or IL-1β. These biological therapies have demonstrated efficacy in suppressing TRAPS symptoms, preventing reactive amyloidosis, and preventing disease progression while mitigating the risk associated with long-term corticosteroid use. Tumor necrosis factor-α blocking agents were introduced based on the observation that soluble p55 TNFR1 was found to be decreased in the serum of patients. Etanercept is beneficial in more than 80% of patients; however, only 30% of patients achieve a complete response. Treatment with etanercept might be efficacious in preventing AA-amyloidosis in some patients. Treatment with infliximab and adalimumab might _trigger_ inflammatory episodes; they are not recommended for the treatment of TRAPS. Anakinra, initially used in TRAPS patients refractory to etanercept medication, has been shown to be efficient in controlling clinical manifestations in TRAPS, normalization of inflammatory markers, and in prevention of disease relapse and complications even when administered on-demand. Anakinra induced complete response in 79% and partial response in 16% of the TRAPS patients in the Eurofever registry. Patients were successfully treated with another IL-1 inhibitor, canakinumab, as demonstrated by complete remissions of all the clinical symptoms and normalization of their inflammatory markers in a randomized controlled trial. In some cases where patients did not respond to TNF or IL-1 inhibitors, treatment with the IL-6 inhibitor tocilizumab was reported as an alternative approach. \n\nRegular monitoring of kidney function and urinalysis to detect the presence of proteinuria serve as valuable \n\nparameters for assessing the clinical activity and response to treatment in individuals with TRAPS. Elevated levels of serum AA protein have been identified as an indicator of amyloid deposition. The prognosis of **AA amyloidosis** , a serious complication associated with TRAPS, is closely tied to the disease activity and effective control of inflammatory attacks is crucial in managing this condition. Amyloidosis represents the most severe complication in untreated TRAPS patients and is associated with an increased risk of morbidity and mortality. Proteinuria and kidney failure are observed in a significant majority (80–90%) of amyloidosis cases, whereas deposits in organs such as the thyroid, myocardium, liver, and spleen are less common. \n\n## **Relopathies/NF-kB Related Autoinflammatory Diseases** \n\nNF-κB is an essential transcription factor that is activated by numerous immunological pathway, such as IL-1 and TNF receptors, Toll-like receptor, and T and B cell receptors. NF-κB activation results in transcription of inflammatory genes, and is also involved in apoptosis. NF-κB is typically a heteroor homodimer made up of several subunits–RelA, RelB, c-Rel, NF-κB1, NF-κB2—in different combinations. A heterodimer consisting of RelA and NF-κB1 is the most frequent active form of NF-κb. The NF-κB subunits are sequestered in the cytoplasm bound to IκBa until properly activated which requires the proteins NEMO, IKKα, and IKKβ. IκBα is phosphorylated by IKKα and IKKβ resulting in its ubiquitination and degradation, releasing the NF-κB subunits which translocate to the nucleus and transcribe NF-κB dependent genes (Fig. 57.4). Several autoinflammatory disorders are associated with defects in this pathway. \n\n## **Haploinsufficiency of A20 (HA20)** \n\nUbiquitination is a posttranslational protein modification and an important mechanism for the regulation of many processes of cell physiology, such as protein degradation or DNA repair. Ubiquitin is removed by deubiquitinases (DUBs), also known as deubiquitinating enzymes. TNFAIP3 or A20 is a DUB with a critical function in the negative regulation of inflammation and immunity. \n\nAn autoinflammatory disease caused by heterozygous loss-of-function variants in _TNFAIP3_ (tumor necrosis factor α-induced protein 3) leads to haploinsufficiency of A20 or TNFAIP3. _TNFAIP3_ is composed of 9 exons on chromosome 6 and encodes a protein that has two main domains, an OTU domain at its N-terminus that plays a key role in the deubiquitinase activity of A20 and a seven zinc finger (7ZF) domains that confers ubiquitinase activity. Most variants are nonsense variants or small frameshift deletions that result in truncation of the OTU or 7ZF domain, thereby impairing the function of the A20 protein. A20 functions as a negative regulator of the NF-κB pathway.\n\n<!-- p.1293 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p1294_01.jpg)\n\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p1293_01.jpg)\n\n**Fig. 57.4** Overview of ubiquitination/deubiquitination within NF-κB pathways. Signaling through NF-κB pathways is regulated by ubiquitination/deubiquitination. Addition of polyubiquitin chains to various pathway components can serve as a mediator for activation while marking others for degradation. LUBAC is an activator of the canonical pathway. It is a complex made up of 3 components: HOIL-1, HOIP, and SHANK-associated RH domain interactor (SHARPIN). On receptor ligation, LUBAC adds polyubiquitin chains to targets, such as NEMO and RIPK1, leading to IKK activation through TAK1. Deubiquitination by enzymes, such as OTULIN and A20, plays a vital role in extinguishing signaling through the NF-κB pathway, maintaining cellular homeostasis. _linear-Ub_ , Met1-linked ubiquitin; _K63-Ub_ , Lys63-linked ubiquitin. (From Scott O, Roifman CM. NF-κ-B pathway and the Goldilocks principle: lessons from human disorders of immunity and inflammation. _J Allergy Clin Immunol_ . 2019;143[5]:1688-1701 [Fig. 2, p. 1691].) \n\nDecreased expression of A20 in patients results in impaired deubiquitination, increased phosphorylation of the inhibitor of nuclear factor kappa b kinase subunit gamma (IKKγ) complex, and increased degradation of the inhibitory protein IκBα. This leads to an activation of the NF-κB pathway, an increased expression of proinflammatory cytokines and systemic inflammation. Failure to downregulate persistent NF-κB signaling in specific cells has been associated with various inflammatory and autoimmune disorders, and malignancies. \n\n**Haploinsufficiency of A20** , a condition characterized by reduced levels of the A20 protein, manifests as an earlyonset systemic inflammation with a wide spectrum of inflammatory and autoimmune features, often varying in severity. It is advisable to consider genetic testing for variants in _TNFAIP3_ (which encodes A20) in individuals who present with a dominantly inherited, early-onset inflammatory disorder that resembles **Behçet disease** or other autoinflammatory and autoimmune diseases, especially those involving mucosal symptoms. Distinguishing features\n\n<!-- p.1294 -->\n\nin individuals with haploinsufficiency of A20 (HA20) include scarring oral ulcers, isolated anterior uveitis or retinal vasculitis with necrotizing inflammation in the eyes, recurrent fevers, severe intestinal inflammation, elevated inflammatory markers, the presence of various autoantibodies that can fluctuate over time, and a disease course that is unresponsive to standard treatments. _These findings are atypical in classic Behçet disease_ . The clinical presentation shows significant heterogeneity. Disease severity varies within families, with some individuals experiencing severe symptoms from an early age, whereas others have milder symptoms that develop later. In the majority of patients, recurrent mucosal ulcers are the initial symptom, leading to an initial misdiagnosis of Behçet disease in half of the patients. \n\nApproximately half of the genetic variants in _TNFAIP3_ have been linked to an elevated risk of various autoimmune and inflammatory disorders. These conditions include juvenile idiopathic arthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, inflammatory bowel disease, systemic lupus erythematosus, type 1 diabetes, psoriasis, celiac disease, and coronary artery disease. Furthermore, A20 may have a role as a tumor suppressor gene; somatic variants and deletions have been identified in B-cell and Hodgkin lymphomas. \n\nImmune cells derived from individuals with HA20 exhibited evidence of increased expression of NF-κB target genes and elevated levels of several proinflammatory cytokines (IL-1β, TNF-α, IL-6, IL-9, IL-17, IL-18, IFN-γ and IFN-γinduced protein-10). During relapses, acute phase reactants are notably elevated. In addition to clinical signs of autoimmunity, approximately half of patients exhibit low-level autoantibodies, including antinuclear antibodies, anti–doublestranded DNA (anti-dsDNA), or lupus anticoagulant. \n\nThere is no standardized treatment for A20 haploinsufficiency due to the wide spectrum of clinical phenotypes and disease severity. Some patients with a milder, _Behçet-like phenotype_ respond to colchicine, whereas others require immune-suppressive drugs including cytokine inhibitors (anti-TNF-α, anti-IL-1). Anticytokine agents such as antiTNF-α or anti-IL-1 have been effective in suppressing the systemic inflammation in most of the patients. Hematopoietic stem cell transplant might be considered in patients with severe and treatment-refractory disease. \n\n## **Otulipenia or OTULIN-Related Autoinflammatory Syndrome (ORAS)** \n\nOTULIN (ovarian tumor deubiquitinase with linear linkage specificity) is a deubiquitinase that interacts with LUBAC (linear ubiquitin chain assembly complex) to remove linear ubiquitin chains, encoded by the _otulin (FAM105B)_ gene. OTULIN removes linear ubiquitin chains from NF-κB essential modulator (NEMO), receptor interacting protein kinase 1 (RIPK1), and A20 to attenuate NF-κB signaling. OTULIN functions as a negative regulator of the canonical NF-κB pathway and as such is essential for resolving \n\ninflammation. Variant cells overproduce many proinflammatory cytokines, including cytokines associated with activation of adaptive immune cells. The _OTULIN (FAM105B)_ gene has 5 exons on chromosome 5. \n\nBiallelic loss-of-function (LoF) variants in _FAM105B_ cause **otulipenia** , also known as **OTULIN-related autoinflammatory syndrome (ORAS)** , an autoinflammatory disease characterized by fevers, neutrophilic dermatitis, panniculitis, arthralgia/arthritis, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, gastrointestinal inflammation, and failure to thrive. Patients affected by this condition typically experience recurrent fevers that last for long periods, along with symptoms like joint swelling, gastrointestinal inflammation with diarrhea and impaired growth. Cutaneous symptoms involve a painful erythematous rash with skin nodules, lipodystrophy, and episodes of pustular rashes in some patients. Skin biopsies reveal evidence of _neutrophilic dermatitis_ , mixed type panniculitis, and inflammation of small and medium-sized blood vessels known as vasculitis. The flares are prolonged and do not resolve without treatment. Heterozygote carriers are asymptomatic, which suggests that reduced protein expression of OTULIN may not be critical for maintenance of immune homeostasis. In contrast to individuals with LUBAC deficiency, patients with OTULIN deficiency do not typically display clear signs of immunodeficiency. However, some of them have experienced iatrogenic infections caused by immunosuppressive treatments. Initial assessments have indicated that these patients exhibit appropriate immune responses to vaccines, adequate proliferation of T and B cells, normal levels of immunoglobulins, and normal counts of T cells, B cells, and NK cells. \n\nOTULIN-related autoinflammatory syndrome (ORAS) can be managed by treatment with the anti-TNFα. with some reports of IL-1 blockers providing benefit. Treatment of affected patients with prednisolone, azathioprine, and methotrexate had little or no effect on the symptoms. Treatment with inhibitors of TNF-α and IL-1 effectively reduces the inflammatory burden in OTULIN deficient patients although disease flares have been noted. The coexistence of NF-κB and interferon signatures in patients with otulipenia is clinically informative for the management of patients who only respond partially to TNF inhibitors. Suppression of interferon signaling by Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors may be therapeutically indicated in patients with the OTULIN deficiency, and they are now frequently used to treat interferonopathies and have been trialed in some patients with ORAS with some benefit. \n\n## **Linear Ubiquitin Chain Assembly Complex (LUBAC) Deficiencies (HOIL-1L/HOIP Deficiency)** \n\nLinear ubiquitin chain assembly complex (LUBAC)mediated ubiquitination is pivotal for regulation of innate and adaptive immune responses and regulation of cell death. Linear ubiquitin chain assembly complex consists of HOIP\n\n<!-- p.1295 -->\n\n(HOIL-1 interacting protein; RNF31) and two accessory proteins: HOIL-1 (heme-oxidized IRP2 ubiquitin ligase 1; RBCK1) and SHARPIN (SHANK-interacting protein like 1; SIPL1). Depletion of any subunits greatly reduces the stability of LUBAC in cells. LUBAC depletion leads to attenuation of NF-κB and the mitogen-activated protein kinases (MAPK)-mediated signaling and increases cell death. **HOIL-1 and HOIP deficiencies** are recessively inherited diseases. Loss of function variants lead to absent NF-κB activity in fibroblasts and B cells (partly explaining the immune deficiency), whereas NF-κB activity was increased in monocytes and was likely responsible for the autoinflammatory manifestations. The disease manifests as systemic autoinflammation with increased propensity for viral and bacterial infections, as well as amylopectinosis and death in early childhood. \n\nIn contrast to immunodeficient fibroblasts, peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) of HOIP and HOIL1-deficient patients are highly responsive to IL-1 stimulation and produce high levels of proinflammatory cytokines IL-6 and MIP-1α. One patient had severe T cell lymphopenia and impaired antibody production. Abnormalities in the lymphatic system **(lymphangiectasia)** have been observed in the HOIP-deficient patient and HOIP-deficient mice, which suggests that HOIP, independently of other LUBAC components, may have a function in the regulation of lymphatic development. \n\nTNF-inhibitory treatment temporarily ameliorated pathology in one HOIL-deficient patient. It is possible that TNF inhibition is a therapeutic approach for patients with LUBAC deficiencies. \n\n## **RelA (p65) Haploinsufficiency** \n\nRelA is a subunit of NF-κB and is encoded by _RELA_ , also known as p65 or NF-κB3. A monogenic disorder phenotypically resembling A20 haploinsufficiency, but with a different underlying abnormality of the NF-κB pathway is RelA haploinsufficiency. RelA haploinsufficiency arises from epithelial and stromal cell intrinsic defects affecting NF-κB activation, resulting in ulceration of mucosal barriers because of cytotoxic effects of TNF. The most common clinical features include orogenital ulceration, rash, fever, gastrointestinal inflammation, and joint inflammation mimicking _Behçet disease_ . Leukocytosis and elevated inflammatory markers, without evidence of infection or autoantibodies, is also seen. Treatment with TNF blockers (infliximab) appears beneficial; IL-1 blockade is ineffective. Studies from patient samples show that fibroblasts, but not lymphocytes, have higher levels of cytotoxicity after TNF administration, suggesting that RelA haploinsufficiency mainly affects stromal cells. Similarly RelA haplosufficient mice developed cutaneous ulceration after TNF exposure, highlighting the protective effects of the RelA subunit and the cytotoxic effects of TNF in stromal cells. Fibroblasts from patients with RelA haploinsufficiency showed increased apoptosis and impaired NF-κB activation in response to TNF. \n\nRelA haploinsufficiency therefore appears to impaired stromal and epithelial cell recovery following a mucosal injury. It is suggested that the systemic autoinflammatory features of this condition might result from effects of the local microbiome on the ulcerated oral, gastrointestinal and vaginal mucosa acting as inflammatory stimuli for further TNF release. \n\n## **ROSAH Syndrome** \n\n**ROSAH (retinal dystrophy, optic nerve edema, splenomegaly, anhidrosis, and migraine headache) syndrome** is an autosomal dominant ocular systemic disorder caused by heterozygous missense variants in alpha kinase 1 _(ALPK1)_ . ALPK1 activation leads to the activation of NF-κB, a pathway known to be altered in other autoinflammatory diseases, including haploinsufficiency of A20 (HA20), haploinsufficiency of RelA, Blau syndrome, and Behçet disease; uveitis is associated with several of these conditions. AntiTNF therapy and other biologics, including anti-IL-1 and anti-IL-6 therapies, have shown partial efficacy. IL-6 directed therapy may be more effective, for ocular features. \n\n# **Other Systemic Autoinflammatory Disorders** \n\n## **Deficiency in Adenosine Deaminase 2** \n\nAdenosine deaminase 2 (ADA2) is a dimeric enzyme that, similarly to its ADA1 isoform, catalyzes the deamination of adenosine and 2′-deoxyadenosine into inosine and deoxyinosine respectively. ADA2 exhibits not only an enzymatic activity, but is proposed to regulate various aspects of innate and adaptive immunity. Deficiency of adenosine deaminase 2 (DADA2) is a rare autosomal recessive monogenic autoinflammatory disease that affects several organs including the skin, blood vessels, B lymphocytes and the bone marrow (Fig. 57.2G). Homozygous or compound heterozygous loss-of-function variants in _CECR1_ is causative (carriers are healthy); variable penetrance and expressivity is noted likely based on the nature of the genetic variants. DADA2 may mimic polyarteritis nodosa (PAN), often with earlyonset stroke and in some cases hematological and immunological abnormalities. Although the pathophysiology of this disorder remains obscure, ADA2 appears to polarize macrophage to a proinflammatory state, known as M1 macrophages, with an increased release of proinflammatory cytokines. TNF-α seems to have a key role in the pathogenesis of the disease. \n\nThe phenotypic spectrum of DADA2 is highly variable including an inflammatory fever syndrome with vasculitis and CNS strokes and a characteristic rash known as livedo racemose or a familial polyarteritis nodosa, or hypogammaglobulinemia, and even hematological manifestations including variable cytopenias and bone marrow failure. The nature of the genetic variants may predict the clinical course with variants that significantly affect the enzymatic function\n\n<!-- p.1296 -->\n\nof ADA2 leading to a more severe phenotype, such as the stroke and/or hematological involvement, whereas a higher enzyme activity has been reported in patients with milder phenotypes or isolated cutaneous involvement. Due to high prevalence of compound heterozygosity a precise genotype–phenotype correlation model has not been established. ADA2 variants with a partial loss of ADA2 activity, namely missense variants, correlate with a vasculitic phenotype whereas complete absent activity was observed in hematological disease. \n\nThe vasculitic features of DADA2 are similar to PAN. The patients typically present with an early-onset stroke, livedoid skin rash, recurrent fevers, skin ulcers, along with some hypogammaglobulinemia, cytopenia, and lymphopenia. Clinical presentations and age at onset vary widely even among related patients. This phenotypic variability can delay the diagnosis of DADA2. The monogenic autoinflammatory disease onset is mainly in childhood but clinical signs and symptoms of DADA2 syndrome may also start in adulthood. Additionally, some of the patients with adult onset might have had episodes and/or moderate signs during childhood that had been misdiagnosed. \n\nDADA2 is diagnosed by demonstrating _ADA2_ variants and/or decreased ADA2 enzyme activity which may predict severity of disease. Heterozygous carriers have enzymatic activity that is usually ~50% of normal, they are generally asymptomatic. However heterozygote DADA2 patients can have symptoms depending on the residual ADA2 activity but generally have milder symptoms than homozygous or compound heterozygous individuals. Thus, analyzing ADA2 enzyme activity is especially critical in deciding for treatment in heterozygotes. Other laboratory abnormalities in DADA2 other than elevated inflammatory markers include a broad spectrum of hematological abnormalities (anemia, neutropenia, lymphopenia, and/or thrombocytopenia) and hypogammaglobulinemia, and these can be progressive over time. \n\nTNF-α inhibitors are the treatment of choice in DADA-2 patients and results effective on controlling the vasculitis signs and preventing the strokes. In patients with humoral immunodeficiency and recurrent infections, immunoglobulin replacement (intravenously or subcutaneously) is used. Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT) can be effective especially in patients with severe hematological abnormalities. Successful treatment with gene therapy to correct ADA2 activity has been demonstrated, however gene therapy for DADA2 is not currently available. \n\n## **NEMO Deleted Exon 5 Autoinflammatory Syndrome** \n\nThe NF-κB essential modulator (NEMO/IKKγ) has a major regulatory role in innate and adaptive immunity. Variants within _NEMO_ , therefore, may cause **immunodeficiency** (anhidrotic ectodermal dysplasia with immunodeficiency) or **inflammatory** phenotypes, depending on the site of the variant. In three male patients, deletion of exon \n\n5 was shown to cause overexpression of NEMO protein leading to strong IFN and NF-κB signature and an autoinflammatory phenotype with features that include fevers, nodular rash, panniculitis, hepatitis, uveitis, chorioretinitis, and optic neuritis. This condition was named **NEMO deleted exon 5 autoinflammatory syndrome (NDAS)** . Unlike diseases arising from other NEMO variants, these patients lack susceptibility to bacterial or viral infections. Despite having high IFN signatures, patients with NDAS do not develop hypertension, basal ganglia calcifications, or white matter disease which can be seen in other interferonopathies. \n\n## **Blau Syndrome** \n\nBlau syndrome is an autosomal dominant disease due to gain of function variants in _NOD2_ . Loss of function variants in _NOD2_ are a risk factor for Crohn disease. _NOD2_ variants in Blau syndrome result in spontaneous activation of the NOD2 protein and activation of NF-κB, and production of proinflammatory cytokines. Blau syndrome is a _granulomatous disease_ classically affecting the skin, joints and eyes tract, and is often diagnosed as early onset sarcoidosis (Fig. 57.2C). Blau syndrome usually begins in early childhood with an erythematous papular rash similar to erythema nodosum, followed by boggy synovitis of the wrist, knees, ankles and proximal interphalangeal joints, and eventually granulomatous uveitis. Other involvement can occur such as tenosynovitis, cranial neuropathies, and large vessel vasculitis can occur. Biopsy of these lesions demonstrates noncaseating granulomas similar to sarcoidosis but respiratory involvement and hilar adenopathy are rare. Laboratory are typically normal, although elevated ESR, immunoglobulin, and angiotensin converting enzyme levels can be seen. Most patients with Blau syndrome are treated with corticosteroids and methotrexate; reports have shown effectiveness of TNF inhibitors (i.e., infliximab). \n\n## **Periodic Fever, Aphthous Stomatitis, Pharyngitis, and Adenitis (PFAPA): The Most Common Nonmonogenic SAID** \n\nA polygenic complex syndrome PFAPA is characterized by periodic fever, aphthous stomatitis, pharyngitis, and cervical adenopathy. Important additional features include the remarkable clockwork periodicity of the attacks, the responsiveness of attacks to abortive doses of corticosteroids (although in some patients the episodes then recur more frequently), and the almost predictable remission that occurs in adolescence. PFAPA exhibit some familial tendency. The disease has a benign course and tends to remit spontaneously with time. Whether PFAPA is triggered by a viral or other infectious agent is not known. \n\nThe disease usually develops before 5 years of age with an incidence estimated at 2.3 per 10,000 children. It is characterized by fever spikes of abrupt onset lasting 3 to 6 days.\n\n<!-- p.1297 -->\n\nSome older children complain of general malaise and myalgia during attacks. The aphthous lesions observed in PFAPA are small, localized to labial gingival tissues, and rapidly self-remitting. Cervical lymph nodes are commonly enlarged and tender. Acute phase reactants and neutrophils are elevated during attacks, and then normalize during symptom-free intervals. Interestingly, children are completely well with normal, linear growth and appropriate development between episodes. \n\nThe diagnosis is PFAPA is primarily based on clinical evaluation (Table 57.2). \n\nComplete blood counts frequently demonstrate neutrophilia during the fever. Inflammatory markers such as CRP or SAA are elevated during the flares and normalize to undetectable levels between febrile episodes, in contrast to some other autoinflammatory disorders or chronic inflammatory conditions. Periodic fever, aphthous stomatitis, pharyngitis, and cervical adenitis syndrome is polygenic; subsets of patients with PFAPA may have heterozygous variants in known autoinflammatory genes including _MEFV_ , _NLRP3_ , _TNFRSF1A_ , and _MVK_ . Patients with PFAPA may have a family history of similar fevers implying an inherited genetic predisposition to the disorder and/or shared environmental exposures. \n\nCorticosteroids are the most effective nonsurgical treatment causing dramatic cessation of fever during attacks, but this does not prevent the future episodes. There are reports that tonsillectomy may also be beneficial. \n"});
